---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { REPO_URL, routes } from '../lib/urls.ts'
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="What the Sandwich Classification Benchmark is, who publishes it, and why it exists."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About this research programme</h1>

    <p>
      The Sandwich Classification Benchmark is an independent, weekly evaluation of how the leading
      commercial language models classify contested items within the sandwich category. Each Monday
      at 12:00 UTC, every enabled model is presented with each research question three times.
      Responses are recorded verbatim, classified, and published without editorial revision.
    </p>

    <h2>Publication</h2>

    <p>
      Editions are published on the Monday of each ISO week and are permanently archived. Prior
      editions are never revised; where a run is repeated within the same week, the later run
      supersedes the earlier one and the edition number is unchanged. The complete
      <a href={routes.history()}>archive</a> is available, as is the underlying data.
    </p>

    <h2>Independence</h2>

    <p>
      No vendor is consulted before publication, sponsors this research, or reviews it in advance.
      No vendor has been asked whether it would like to be included. The complete source code, the
      raw data behind every figure, and the full methodology are
      <a href={REPO_URL}>public</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>

    <p>
      Errors of fact are corrected by amending the source data and republishing, with the change
      recorded in version control. Disagreements with our classification rules — which are published
      in full on the <a href={routes.methodology()}>methodology</a> page — are welcome as issues.
    </p>

    <h2>What this actually is</h2>

    <p>
      This is a teaching project, and the deadpan is the joke. Whether a hot dog is a sandwich is
      not a serious research question, and nothing here should be mistaken for a real evaluation of
      model quality.
    </p>

    <p>
      Everything <em>around</em> the question is sincere. Building a working cross-provider benchmark
      means solving a specific set of unglamorous problems: several vendors with several wire formats,
      token counts that do not mean the same thing across providers, latency that depends on where your
      runner happens to be, models that answer differently on Tuesday than they did on Monday, and one
      provider having an outage in the middle of your data collection. This repository solves those problems
      in the smallest honest way and documents how, so that you can throw away the hot dog and point it
      at a question you actually care about.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you want the serious version: read the
      <a href={routes.howItWorks()}>how it works</a> page, swap the contents of
      <code>questions.json</code>, and you have a benchmark.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
